  "worked_solution": "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Since $A$ is upper-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\u223c[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, we have $E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, the change of variables that decouples the system is given by $x(t)=Eu(t)$, or equivalently,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)={u}_{1}(t)-{u}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)={u}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
